e unit vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "946d71c0-c7c2-59d9-aaa7-5ecb48201289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432330 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, 